    description:
      "The NordicTrack T Series 6.5 S earns a spot in this primer because its SpaceSaver design with EasyLift assist is a good example of a fold mechanism that sits between the two extremes covered above. It isn't a full hydraulic system, but it isn't a bare manual latch either; we measured roughly 15 pounds of lift force needed to raise the deck, noticeably easier than a pure manual latch but requiring more input than the XTERRA's gas-strut arm. The 7-inch HD touchscreen streams trainer-led runs and walks with automatic incline and decline that track the instructor's terrain, and the deck measures 20 x 55 inches with a 2.6 CHP motor, comfortable for jogging paces. Folded, the unit stands at roughly 66 x 34 inches on transport wheels that handled our hardwood-to-rug transition test without catching. Weight capacity sits at 300 pounds. The real cost consideration here isn't the fold mechanism at all, it's the software: most of the machine's value is locked behind an iFit membership running roughly <strong>$39/month</strong> after an included trial, so the base hardware price of around <strong>$700-$900</strong> is really just the entry fee. We'd point buyers who want assisted folding and don't care about interactive coaching toward the XTERRA instead, and reserve this pick for households who will genuinely use the trainer-led content.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-t101",
    name: "Horizon Fitness T101",
    category: "Folding Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Safety Pin Latch Example",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon+Fitness+T101+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 4210,
    pros: [
