      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          "Folding treadmill" gets used as if it describes one kind of
          product, but the term actually covers machines that behave
          completely differently once you're the one lifting the deck. A
          gas-strut hinge and a bare-bones manual latch both technically
          "fold," yet one takes a light one-handed push and the other asks
          you to muscle 20-plus pounds of deck upward every single day. This
          guide exists to explain those differences in plain language before
          you ever open a product listing, so you know exactly what
          mechanism you're buying and why it matters.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="category-overview" class="scroll-mt-24">
          What "Folding Treadmill" Actually Covers
        </h2>
        <p>
          The folding treadmill category spans an enormous range, from
          $200 ultra-compact walking pads meant for a dorm room to
          $1,000-plus full-deck machines that fold primarily as a
          convenience rather than a necessity. What unites them is a hinge
          somewhere in the frame that lets the deck pivot upward, usually
          toward the console, so the whole unit can stand vertically and
          take up a fraction of its unfolded floor space. Beyond that shared
          trait, folding treadmills diverge sharply in deck size, motor
          power, weight capacity, and, most importantly for this guide, in
          exactly how that fold happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth being precise about terminology here because
          manufacturers aren't always consistent. Some list a treadmill as
          "foldable" when it technically requires two people and a fair
          amount of force to lift the deck; others use the same word for a
          machine with a smooth, spring-assisted hinge that a single person
          operates with one hand. The spec sheet rarely spells out which
          category a given machine falls into, which is exactly the gap
          this primer is meant to fill. Once you understand the three broad
          mechanism types, you can look past marketing language and
          evaluate a listing on its actual folding experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also helps to understand who folding treadmills are actually
          built for, since that shapes which mechanism matters most to you.
          Renters and apartment dwellers who fold and unfold daily care
          most about ease of use and speed. Buyers in a dedicated but
          small home gym who fold only occasionally, say once a week to
          vacuum, care less about daily convenience and more about frame
          rigidity and deck size while unfolded. And buyers choosing a
          folding model purely for portability between rooms or between
          homes prioritize weight and folded footprint over anything else.
          None of these buyers are wrong to want different things, but
          conflating them leads to disappointment, which is why the
          mechanism breakdown below is organized around effort and
          frequency of use rather than price alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more distinction worth flagging early: folding capability is
          separate from portability. A treadmill can fold compactly and
          still be difficult to move between rooms if it's heavy or its
          transport wheels are small and hard to roll across carpet.
          Conversely, a treadmill can be relatively light and easy to
          reposition even with a folded footprint that isn't the smallest
          in its class. If your goal is moving the machine between a home
          office and a garage seasonally, weigh both fold mechanism and
          total weight, not just the folded dimensions printed on the box.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
