      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="what-to-look-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          What to Look For Beyond the Fold Itself
        </h2>
        <p>
          Understanding the fold mechanism is the first step, but a
          treadmill that folds beautifully and rides poorly isn't a good
          buy either. Once you've narrowed down which mechanism type fits
          your household, a few other details deserve equal attention.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deck size matters more than folded footprint for anyone who runs
          rather than walks. A shorter deck, common on ultra-compact
          folding models, can feel fine at a walking pace and cramped the
          moment you pick up to a jog, since your stride naturally
          lengthens with speed. If running is even an occasional part of
          your routine, look for a deck at least 20 inches wide and 55
          inches long, and be skeptical of decks meaningfully smaller than
          that regardless of how appealing the folded dimensions look on a
          product page.
        </p>
        <p>
          Weight capacity deserves a buffer, not a ceiling you plan to
          approach. We'd recommend staying at least 20-30 pounds under a
          machine's stated capacity, since that number reflects an upper
          bound under ideal conditions rather than a comfortable long-term
          operating range. This matters more on folding treadmills
          specifically, since lighter frame construction is often part of
          how manufacturers achieve a smaller folded footprint in the
          first place.
        </p>
        <p>
          Transport wheels are easy to overlook but make a real difference
          in daily use. Larger wheels roll more easily across carpet and
          handle small threshold transitions without catching, while
          smaller wheels, common on ultra-compact models, can require
          tilting the unit back further or lifting slightly to clear a rug
          edge. If your fold-and-store routine involves moving the machine
          across different flooring types daily, factor wheel size into
          your decision alongside the fold mechanism itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, consider how the fold mechanism ages. Hydraulic struts
          can lose pressure gradually over years of heavy use, though
          quality units are rated for many thousands of cycles and
          typically outlast the rest of the machine. Manual latches can
          develop looseness at the pivot point if never lubricated.
          Neither issue is common in the first few years of ownership, but
          if you're comparing warranty terms, check specifically whether
          the frame and fold mechanism are covered separately from
          electronics, since that's usually where the longest coverage
          period applies.
        </p>

