        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What's the difference between a hydraulic and a manual latch fold?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              A hydraulic or gas-strut mechanism uses a spring-loaded arm to
              do most of the lifting and to control the deck's descent, so
              folding takes a light push and lowering is slow and
              controlled. A manual latch relies on you lifting the deck by
              hand until a pin or latch engages, which takes more physical
              effort but is perfectly safe when properly engaged and
              inspected periodically.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Are motorized fold treadmills worth the extra cost?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For most buyers, no, unless limited strength or mobility
              makes even a hydraulic-assist fold difficult. Motorized
              folding removes physical effort almost entirely, but it adds
              cost and one more electronic component that can eventually
              need service, so we'd reserve it for shoppers with a
              specific accessibility need.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Does a smaller folded footprint always mean a worse treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Not necessarily, but there's usually a trade-off.
              Ultra-compact folding treadmills often achieve their small
              size through lighter frame materials and shorter decks,
              which can cap weight capacity and running comfort. If you
              mainly walk and have limited storage, a compact machine is a
              fine trade; if you run regularly, prioritize deck length
              even if it costs some folded footprint.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How do I know if a folding mechanism is safe for kids and pets?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Look specifically for a secondary safety pin or lock in
              addition to the primary latch or hydraulic strut. This
              second stage prevents the deck from dropping even if the
              primary lock isn't fully engaged, which matters most in
              households where small children or pets might bump into a
              folded, upright unit.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Should I read your full tested rankings before buying a folding treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. This page is meant as an educational primer on how
              folding mechanisms work and what to look for. For
              side-by-side scoring, longer-term durability notes, and a
              ranked list of seven models we tested over multiple weeks,
              our
              <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">full folding treadmill guide</a>
              goes much deeper into the buying decision itself.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do folding treadmills need more maintenance than fixed-deck models?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Only slightly, and mostly limited to the hinge itself. A
              periodic visual check for looseness or unusual noise, plus
              an occasional light dry lubricant on the hinge pins, is
              usually all that's needed. Belt tension and lubrication
              schedules are the same as on any treadmill and matter more
              to long-term reliability than the fold mechanism does.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
